the Supreme Court on the final day of  its term has just issued two  long-expected rulings first the High  Court decided it cannot get involved in  a dispute over congressional district  maps that were challenged as being too  favorable to one political party and the  court ruled the Trump administration  needs to explain more fully why it wants  to add a question about citizenship to  the 2020 census until then it could not  be added Jan Crawford is outside the  Supreme Court she's been looking over  the document Jan what are the justices  saying in this sense is ruling well I  mean I think the bottom line in the  census case is that it's not over the  justices and a majority opinion by the  Chief Justice John Roberts joined by the  four liberal justices are saying they're  just not buying the explanation that the  Commerce Department gave for why it  needed to add that citizenship question  remember they said that the Justice  Department needed the citizenship  information so that they could properly  enforce the Voting Rights Act but the  court in its decision says look we're  not buying that I mean there's a lot of  evidence that you cooked up that  explanation after the fact after you  already decided that that question  should be in the census this does not  mean there won't be a citizenship  question in the census it just means the  Commerce Department is going to have to  do a better job explaining why they want  one in the first place the question  though is time the Census Bureau said it  needed an answer on this by the end of  this month  obviously that's only a few days away so  there's a question of whether or not  practically they can include it in the  census but remember the census is going  to be online this year for the first  time or 2020 for the first time so it's  unclear actually how much time they're  going to need but right now it's not  over they're going to need some more  information Jen on gerrymandering the  justices by a 5-4 vote decided  redistricting cannot be policed by a  federal court what does that mean again  a decision by the Chief Justice this  time with the conservatives saying that  this isn't a question for the federal  courts to get involved in when a state  state legislatures are drawing their  electoral electoral maps that if they're  doing it purely for political purposes  to preserve their own power the federal  courts shouldn't be getting involved in  that state courts can look at it state  legislators can come up with independent  commissions for redistricting but short  of something like race discrimination  there  it's not going to get involved in these  issues that they see as purely political  disputes a very stron dissent in this  case by the way by justice Elena Kagan  with the Liberals in which she  summarized from the bench calling it a  really a sad day for democracy very  interesting Jen thank you very much
 

